    "q-139477": {
      "dom_id": "step-q139477",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}9 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Equivalently, it is the acute angle between $x$ and any non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,2\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle.",
